WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.650
Every prepared row retains
its record identifier,

2
00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.190
target time and forecast
issue time as metadata.

3
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:12.480
Training and validation then contain the
same forty five ordered feature columns

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.970
and a separate target.

5
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.570
The test export has identity and features,
with targets omitted.

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.370
The original source remains retained
for eventual final evaluation.

7
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.340
These contracts imply feature shapes of
eight thousand six hundred forty five by

8
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:29.810
forty five,

9
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.210
four thousand three hundred
fifty seven by forty five,

10
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.430
and four thousand three hundred
seventy five by forty five.

11
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.360
Shape and schema preparation do not
themselves create training arrays or fit a

12
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.210
demand model.

13
00:00:45.109 --> 00:00:48.979
The calendar schema records input fields,
category definitions

14
00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:50.279
and feature order.

15
00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:54.959
Membership records each observation's
issue time and assigned partition,

16
00:00:55.089 --> 00:00:56.579
including exclusions.

17
00:00:57.509 --> 00:00:59.419
The split manifest records windows,

18
00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:00.739
timing assumptions,

19
00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:02.319
counts and hashes.

20
00:01:03.209 --> 00:01:05.479
Hashes identify our retained bytes;

21
00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:07.779
they are not official signed checksums.

22
00:01:08.289 --> 00:01:10.659
Repeating preparation
checks the same files.

23
00:01:11.129 --> 00:01:12.759
If a frozen artifact changes,

24
00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:16.779
the command raises an error instead
of quietly redefining the dataset.

25
00:01:17.349 --> 00:01:21.409
This keeps later model comparisons
attached to the same observations and

26
00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:22.319
feature meanings.

27
00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:28.769
These two commands run from the
Bike Demand Planner project folder.

28
00:01:29.399 --> 00:01:32.889
The first acquires or
verifies the retained source,

29
00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:36.609
audits the rows and writes
the frozen calendar exports.

30
00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:41.959
The second follows the Monday training
observation through the existing parser

31
00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:42.969
and encoder.

32
00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:45.729
Its output includes record fifty four,

33
00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:50.269
a seven o'clock issue time, forty
five features and the separate target.

34
00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:53.829
The four active names
match the vector we traced.

35
00:01:54.379 --> 00:01:57.989
You can revisit this runnable example
while reviewing the explanation.

36
00:02:00.109 --> 00:02:03.319
The calendar example
prints record fifty four,

37
00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:08.639
its seven o'clock issue time, training
membership and four active inputs.

38
00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:14.489
A changed hour probe replaces hour eight
with hour nine while retaining the other

39
00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:14.979
groups.

40
00:02:15.749 --> 00:02:19.879
Supplying the target to the
encoder fails its exact field rule.

41
00:02:20.509 --> 00:02:23.939
A twenty thirteen target
fails the supported date rule.

42
00:02:24.809 --> 00:02:28.399
The data handoff passed twenty
two checks before commit.

43
00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:31.679
Perturbing held out
outcomes left training,

44
00:02:32.169 --> 00:02:33.879
schema and membership unchanged.

45
00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:36.579
These are implementation checks.

46
00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:40.579
They do not establish model
accuracy or learner understanding.

47
00:02:42.609 --> 00:02:44.859
Consider three changed conditions.

48
00:02:45.929 --> 00:02:48.959
First, move the hour from eight to nine:

49
00:02:49.289 --> 00:02:53.459
only the corresponding hour indicators
change in this controlled probe.

50
00:02:54.229 --> 00:02:57.339
Second, add thirty minutes
of reporting delay:

51
00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:02.099
midnight is no longer a ready issue
time for the newly completed label.

52
00:03:02.729 --> 00:03:05.599
The earliest allowed
hourly issue moves later.

53
00:03:06.809 --> 00:03:10.559
Third, request the previous
clock hour after a source gap.

54
00:03:10.929 --> 00:03:13.699
The previous row might
be two hours earlier.

55
00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:17.219
Each case asks the same
practical question:

56
00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:20.999
which information is available
for this particular forecast?

57
00:03:21.749 --> 00:03:26.679
The timeline, feature schema and source
identities make that question testable.

58
00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:34.027
We have followed one real hour from its
source row to fixed calendar inputs and

59
00:03:34.027 --> 00:03:35.617
chronological membership.

60
00:03:36.047 --> 00:03:39.357
The important boundaries are
identity versus features,

61
00:03:39.947 --> 00:03:42.537
later outcomes versus available inputs,

62
00:03:43.027 --> 00:03:45.937
and label readiness versus
forecast issue time.

63
00:03:46.407 --> 00:03:51.817
The completed data preparation
is committed as ad1b870.

64
00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:57.557
The workspace's later constant baseline
lives in a separate learning record.

65
00:03:58.187 --> 00:04:03.057
This tutorial explains the data handoff
and preserves the final test boundary.

66
00:04:03.647 --> 00:04:08.527
Future predictors can now be compared
using the same recorded hours and the same

67
00:04:08.527 --> 00:04:09.897
explicit assumptions.

68
00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.510
A reproducible prepared row
has more than one identity.

69
00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.830
The retained C S V hash
identifies exact bytes,

70
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:27.260
so changing line endings can change the
hash even when parsed numbers appear the

71
00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:27.790
same.

72
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.650
The schema identifies
feature meanings and order.

73
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.540
Membership connects observation
identifiers and timestamps to their

74
00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.150
partitions.

75
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.130
These checks answer different questions.

76
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.320
A row count or a familiar filename alone
does not establish that a later run used

77
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.810
the same data contract.

78
00:04:50.313 --> 00:04:53.903
Replay the running observation
as a sequence of checks.

79
00:04:54.153 --> 00:04:56.183
Locate record fifty four,

80
00:04:56.793 --> 00:05:00.563
confirm its eight o’clock target
label and seven o’clock issue,

81
00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:03.323
and select the named calendar fields.

82
00:05:03.973 --> 00:05:07.623
Encoding gives the active positions eight,
twenty five,

83
00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:09.463
thirty one and forty four.

84
00:05:10.593 --> 00:05:12.563
Compare their meanings
with the saved schema.

85
00:05:13.453 --> 00:05:16.903
The rental total remains separate
from those forty five values.

86
00:05:17.493 --> 00:05:20.143
This trace establishes
a prepared observation.

87
00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:23.923
It does not fit a coefficient
or measure generalization.

88
00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:27.917
If all these checks pass,

89
00:05:28.287 --> 00:05:29.437
what can we claim?

90
00:05:30.127 --> 00:05:34.157
Pause before turning preparation
evidence into a model result.

91
00:05:35.007 --> 00:05:38.097
We can claim that the prepared
observation, representation

92
00:05:38.747 --> 00:05:42.157
and partition identities agree
with their declared contracts.

93
00:05:42.627 --> 00:05:45.897
We cannot claim that a
demand predictor is accurate,

94
00:05:46.187 --> 00:05:48.317
that the learner has
mastered the material,

95
00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:51.117
or that hosted recovery has succeeded.

96
00:05:51.727 --> 00:05:55.547
The next baseline should use the same
frozen observations and validation

97
00:05:55.547 --> 00:05:56.457
contract.

98
00:05:56.867 --> 00:06:00.157
The reserved final test
remains outside tuning.
